I want to understand the structure of a large language model. From what I have understood is that there are 2 processes. One. Was it to. Converts all the words into tokens. And then those tokens are stored as vectors. Number one, Number 2, what I've understood is that uh, The relationship between words is also stored in a weight. And then During influence, it just sees which is the most probable relationship which we user is expecting. Is it something like this? Explain to me in simple words.
You're absolutely on the right track! That is a fantastic high-level summary. Let me clean it up and fill in the gaps using a simple analogy so it all clicks together.

Here is the simple, step-by-step truth of how an LLM works:

1. The "Dictionary & Translator" Step (Your first point)
You are correct. The model cannot read letters; it only understands numbers.

Tokenization = chopping your sentence into small chunks (words or parts of words).

Vectorization = turning each chunk into a long list of numbers (a vector).

Think of it like this: If you speak English and the model speaks Math, this step is the translator that turns your English into Math so the model can think.

2. The "Brain" Step (Your second point)
You are also correct about the weights! But let's refine what they are.

The weights are not just "relationships." They are the model's "experience" or "memory."

During training, the model read billions of pages of text. Every time it read a sentence, it adjusted these weights like knobs on a giant mixing board. By the end of training, these knobs were perfectly tuned so that the model knows patterns (e.g., "rain" usually pairs with "wet," and "king" pairs with "queen").

3. The "Prediction" Step (The missing piece)
This is the most important part: An LLM does NOT "know" answers. It is a pure prediction machine.

When you type a prompt, here is what actually happens inside:

The model takes your vectorized words and runs them through its billions of weights.

It does a massive amount of math to calculate: "Given the words the user just typed, what is the very next word that is most likely to appear?"

It picks that word, adds it to your sentence, and then asks itself the exact same question again with the new, longer sentence.

It repeats this loop (predict → add word → predict → add word) until it decides to stop.

Putting it all together in one analogy:

Imagine you are playing a "Guess the next word" game with a friend who has read every book in the world.

Step 1: You say "The sky is..." (Tokenization)

Step 2: Your friend's brain (the weights) has read millions of sentences. He knows that after "The sky is," the words "blue" or "clear" appear 90% of the time, while "purple" appears only 1% of the time.

Step 3: He doesn't "think" about the answer. He just calculates the probabilities and shouts the most likely word: "blue."

Then he takes "The sky is blue" and predicts the next word: "." (period). Then he stops.

So, to directly answer your question:
Yes, it converts words to vectors. Yes, it stores relationships as weights. But during use (inference), it is not searching its memory for an answer. It is rolling a heavily weighted dice billions of times, one word at a time, to write a sentence that mathematically fits your prompt.

The magic is that by predicting words so well, it accidentally creates coherent sentences, answers questions, and even "reasons" – even though it has no actual intelligence, just really, really good math for guessing the next word.

Based on our conversation, here is a clear, non-technical definition of your project's broader vision.

---

🎯 Project Vision & Purpose

The Big Idea

Build a reasoning engine that understands language the way humans do—through rules, relationships, and logic—not just by matching patterns or predicting the next word.

Why This Matters

Current AI systems (like ChatGPT) are impressive but fundamentally don't "understand" anything. They:

· Memorize patterns from vast amounts of text
· Cannot truly reason or explain their thinking
· Often make logical errors that a child wouldn't make

Your project aims to build an alternative path: a system that genuinely understands meaning by encoding language as formal logic, enabling it to think, reason, and explain its conclusions.

---

🌟 Core Purpose

Primary Goal

Create a language understanding system where:

1. Meaning is formalized — Each word maps to a logical concept with precise relationships
2. Grammar is structure — Sentence patterns define how concepts combine and relate
3. Reasoning is automatic — The system derives new knowledge from what it already knows
4. Explanations are possible — Every answer comes with a traceable chain of reasoning

What This Enables

· Trustworthy AI — You can verify why the system gave a particular answer
· Teachability — You can add new rules and the system learns instantly (no retraining)
· Precision — No hallucinations or made-up facts, just logical conclusions
· Transparency — The system shows its work, unlike black-box neural networks

---

🗺️ Broader Scope & Ambition

What This Project IS About

Aspect Focus
Core Philosophy Language as logic, meaning as structure
Approach Symbolic AI, formal grammar, logical reasoning
Key Activity Translating natural language into formal mathematical expressions
Primary Output A reasoning system that can answer questions with proof
Domain Knowledge representation and automated reasoning

What This Project IS NOT About

Aspect Exclusion
Technology No neural networks, deep learning, or LLMs
Data No training data, scraping, or massive datasets
Features No PDF ingestion, document search, or web crawling
Scale Not aiming for large-scale knowledge bases or commercial deployment
Complexity Not handling messy, ambiguous, or ungrammatical language

The Long-Term Vision

Imagine a system where you could:

· Teach it a domain (e.g., an application's user manual, a company's policy document, a scientific paper)
· Ask it questions that require connecting multiple pieces of information
· Receive answers with clear reasoning you can follow and verify
· Add new rules and see the system immediately incorporate them into its reasoning

This is the dream of explainable AI — a system whose intelligence is fully transparent and controllable.

---

🎪 Success Beyond Technology

What Success Looks Like

Dimension Success Indicator
Conceptual A working prototype that proves language → logic → reasoning is feasible
Demonstrative A clear demo showing step-by-step reasoning for 5-10 test cases
Educational A project that makes formal semantics accessible and tangible
Foundational A solid base for others to build upon and extend
Philosophical A concrete example of how meaning can be formalized

What This Project Achieves

· Proof of Concept — Demonstrates that natural language can be systematically translated to logic
· Educational Value — Makes abstract concepts (First-Order Logic, Montague Semantics) concrete
· Foundation — Provides a base that can be extended to handle more complex language
· Alternative Vision — Shows that "understanding" in AI can be achieved through rules, not just data

---

🧭 Guiding Principles

Principle Meaning
Meaning First Every word matters; every sentence has a precise logical interpretation
Compositional The meaning of the whole comes from the meaning of its parts
Truth-Conditional Understanding means knowing what makes a statement true or false
Rule-Based Knowledge is encoded as rules, not statistical patterns
Explainable Every conclusion must be traceable back to premises
Extensible Add new words and rules without disrupting existing ones
Demonstrative Show, not just tell — have a working demo that proves the concept

---

📚 Intellectual Context

This project stands on the shoulders of giants:

Framework Contribution
Montague Semantics Natural language can be treated with the same formal rigor as logic
First-Order Logic A mathematical language for representing facts and reasoning
Compositional Semantics Meaning is built from the meanings of constituent parts
Automated Theorem Proving Machines can derive new knowledge from logical premises
Lexical Functional Grammar Syntax and semantics are separate but interacting layers

The Broader Conversation

Your project aligns with the long-standing debate in AI:

· Symbolic AI — Intelligence comes from manipulating symbols according to rules (this project)
· Connectionist AI — Intelligence emerges from patterns in neural networks (modern LLMs)

Your project champions the symbolic approach, demonstrating its power and elegance.

---

🏁 Ultimate Purpose

In One Sentence

To prove that a machine can understand language by treating it as formal logic.

More Specifically

To build a system that:

· Takes structured English input
· Translates it into mathematical logic
· Reasons about it automatically
· Answers questions with transparent, verifiable reasoning

Why This Matters

